commercial banks for example are not  going to uh violate the sanctions  because that would put at risk their own  access to the International Financial  system so those aren't the kinds of  things we should be looking for but  maybe smaller Chinese Banks or China  lending parts of its reserves or maybe  the Chinese policy Banks like the  development bank or the ex-in bank could  provide loans directly to Russian  Enterprises and not have to go through  the banks so there are things that China  can do and I think the Biden  Administration is trying to discourage  China from doing anything to help Russia  even if it is uh not in direct violation  of the sanctions and that's where of  course we've heard from Beijing that  they said they will conduct trade  Financial interactions  with with Russia as as as normal  um but words are one thing and then  actions are something else and and so  we'll have to see again what are the  decisions China makes does China want to  be to basically throw in its lot with  Russia  um or does it want to continue to  balance these interests and try to  preserve its relationship not just with  the United States but importantly with  Europe and I think that China should see  Europe as a very important player going  forward because China wants to continue  to have the trade and economic ties that  it has with Europe and also I think sees  Europe as an important poll in the  International System that may not always  be aligned with the United States so we  have an important EU China Summit coming  up on April 1st and and I think that  that's another development to watch  going forward  now of course we've talked about the the  various areas in which China could  support Russia the military field is  obviously particularly sensitive  um if China did start providing some  kind of Military Support what would that  mean like how big a step would that be  if China went ahead with that  it would be huge  if the ukrainians uncovered for example  a ballistic or cruise missile that has  Chinese markings on it that had been  fired by Russia  and I think that that would really be a  game changer in China's relationship  with Russia if they provided Munitions  that were being used uh to Target uh  things like hospitals  um and and maternity uh Awards uh this  is the kind of thing we've seen reported  that the Russians are targeting  civilians and if the Chinese are  complicit I think that that would just  be enormously consequential in a very  negative way for China's relationship  with the rest of the world  so what are we talking about are we  talking about you know a kind of a huge  new Cold War scenario the the cleaving  of the world into two blocks would we  have to talk about the war on you in  Ukraine as a proxy war between the west  and China  I think that's exactly right I think  increasingly Ukraine would begin to look  like a a proxy war  um and that this cold war that everybody  has been denying could ever come about  not not of course exactly like the U.S  Soviet Cold War but a new version of it  that we would have to say yes that's the  new world we are entering  let's think a little bit in the other  direction what do you think it would  take for China to drop Putin  we've seen Putin taking very very risky  actions we've seen Putin and his forces  targeting civilians  at what point do you think it just gets  all too much uh for the Chinese to  stomach are we talking about if it veers  into the use of a chemical or tactical  nuclear weapons or or is it just more  scenes like we've seen from Mario Paul  for instance  well I think more scenes like what we  have uh seen from uh Mario poll I think  it's not enough uh to change China's uh  stance  however if the Russians were to use  chemical uh biological weapons God  forbid nuclear weapons yes  um I think that that would really cause  the Chinese to reconsider  the downsides of sign of siding so  closely uh with Moscow I think they they  already as I said feel a bit a bit  uncomfortable  but that would I think just be over the  top uh for for China I don't think that  that's something that they would be able  to ignore uh but even without such a  catastrophic developments there is the  possibility of some understanding  between the United States and China and  if Xi Jinping is looking for  um some way to perhaps distance himself  a little bit from Moscow I don't think  he would cut off his relationship  completely but if he sees that it's in  China's interests I think that's what  some people hope is that he will if we  give him uh the opportunity uh to back  away a bit that he will do so I believe  that this is a proposition we probably  should test we have uh responsibility uh  to the people of Ukraine and to the rest  of the world to test the proposition  that China is not willing to continue  down this path of siding with the rep  apprehensible actions that Russia is  taking against Ukraine  I mean what's the the smartest approach  in your view from the U.S standpoint but  also for the Europeans I mean we we've  had particularly the US there's there's  been a bipartisan kind of grow growing  bipartisan consensus that the United  States has to push back very hard  against China that it has to Rally  allies to stand up against China as the  predominant um authoritarian power in  the world  do you see voices becoming louder in  saying well actually the West really has  to try to hold or reach out to China to  get it on site against Russia and this  being a kind of a flip of a debate that  was happening previously about whether  the West had to reach out to Russia to  peel it away from China  the right strategy is at this point to  peel China away but perhaps to paint for  Beijing the two different Pathways that  they face  uh one of course is uh that Xi Jinping  decides that he has cited too closely  with Moscow that he backs away a bit  that um perhaps really does take a  neutral stance agrees to abide by all of  the sanctions  um even if China is unwilling to play a  role in encouraging Putin to back off  that it stops playing an unhelpful role  and begins to signal that it is time to  bring an end to the war  um the other pathway for China of course  is is a negative one and and it is one  in which the United States and Europe  must clearly signal to China that if it  helps Russia uh in its um uh in its in  its military efforts or if it aids the  Russian economy that it will face uh  punitive measures from the United States  and Europe and let's be clear that if  China is not compliant with the  sanctions those measures should include  secondary sanctions on China  and this is a conversation that the US  and Europe I hope are already having and  I hope that we can be on the same page  because we all know that imposing  sanctions on China is very different  than imposing sanctions on Russia  if China doesn't tip so far in that  direction  um you mentioned earlier that China's  showed no signs so far of attempting to  mediate but is it to you conceivable  that some kind of mediation role could  emerge for China  particularly given that it is one of the  few countries that might have some  influence on Russia's Behavior  I do not think that we should support  China playing the role as mediator  because China's not an uh  are disinterested uh  um actor it is not neutral  it has very strong interests I believe  in having Russia not be weakened by this  war and seeing an outcome that protects  Russia's vital interest  so I do not think that China  should be in a position of playing the  role of mediator that said if Xi Jinping  has a private conversation with Putin um  and wants to encourage him to  um  agree to more substantive negotiations  and to actually reach a ceasefire um and  and and some negotiated outcome I'm sure  everybody would welcome that but I just  don't think that China is a neutral  Observer I do not think it should be  playing the role of of mediator and  there are other countries that have  stepped up such as Israel who probably  um are far better suited and far better  equipped to play this kind of role  just a final question in your expertise  you focus a lot on Taiwan over the years  um there's been a lot of talk of  comparisons between this situation and  and China's desire to to take control of  Taiwan a lot of talk about the  differences as well um between the  scenarios  um what lessons do you feel China will  be learning as this crisis plays out  with a view to its aspirations on Taiwan  we know that China studies previous Wars  draws lessons from them uh the Chinese  military certainly studied the the Gulf  War and applied some of those Lessons  Learned to its own modernization and  planning in this case uh the war in  Ukraine probably does carry some lessons  for China but the war is not over yet so  the we cannot be clear about what  lessons that it will learn but at least  from what we have seen so far I think we  should expect that China will draw uh  some lessons from the strength of the  Ukrainian resistance and recognize that  even a country with a very strong  conventional military Advantage such as  Russia has over Ukraine does not mean  that Victory can be easily secured and  of course the other side of that coin is  that Taiwan should learn the lesson  itself that it needs to prepare a  civilian resistance because Taiwan does  not have a territorial Defense Force  like Ukraine has and Taiwan is beginning  to bolster its reserves but it really  has not developed a a civilian Defense  Force so uh that's interactive the  lessons that Taiwan and China themselves  might might draw I think that China will  also draw some lessons from the unity of  the United States and Europe and and  Japan South Korea so many countries  around the world that have been willing  to uh to put sanctions on on Russia  again China is is a different case than  Russia and there may not be as much uh  willingness to impose sanctions on on  China but but China's expectation that  these alliances of the United States are  fraying  um and that the the East is rising the  West is declining um and they I think  that these are wrong analytic judgments  that China has made so I hope that they  uh as part of these lessons will will  rethink uh the trends in the in the  global of global balance of power and  their own power will it to other  countries one lesson I think China will  not draw  um some people have said that because  the United States did not come to  Ukraine's help militarily that it would  therefore not come to taiwan's Aid uh  militarily and and this is  disinformation that Beijing actually is  telling Taiwan the United States is  going to abandon you I I don't think  that China will draw that lesson at all  the Chinese understand  um that Ukraine is very different uh  than uh than Taiwan is and let's be  clear that President Biden even before  the conflict started made it made it  clear that the United States was not  going to enter this this war militarily  if Russia invaded But we still uh  continue to have a policy of strategic  ambiguity uh which increasingly I think  is a likelihood that the United States  would intervene although we do not give  Taiwan an ironclad commitment that we  would defend it but I think given the uh  the growing competition uh between the  United States and China that it is um  almost  um inevitable that the United States  would come to taiwan's defense so I  think that China does have to factor  that in but they will watch this  military operation and uh the future of  U.S alliances and see how uh the U.S and  its allies are willing to incur some  costs themselves and maybe they would do  the same if uh China were to attack  Taiwan so they will study this for some  time to come I do not believe that  Beijing is um has already made a  decision that it must use Force against  Taiwan  um I think that they are quite confident  in their large toolkit for 